    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've heard some people say that plateaus feel like something is actively working against you, almost like it's being sabotaged. from a physiological standpoint, why does it feel like progress just stops even when you're putting in the work?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Plateaus occur because the body has adapted to your current stimulus, meaning neural adaptations and muscle strength have reached a temporary equilibrium, so continued effort no longer yields new muscle fiber recruitment or force production. This feeling of stagnation often stems from reaching the \"local maximum\" of a specific skill or strength trait without an external variable, like a change in intensity, to push the adaptation curve upward. Biologically, it feels like sabotage because the peripheral nervous system is resistant to further increases in neural drive required for heavier loads until recovery or stress is reduced. Ultimately, the brain signals no further immediate gain when current training variables no longer provide a sufficient challenge to the adaptive system.",
      "source": "target",
