    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you dive a bit more into that \"local maximum\" idea? if the body reaches that equilibrium where it just refuses to adapt, is there a specific way to tell if it's just fatigue or if their central nervous system is actually hitting a hard ceiling? how do we distinguish between needing more rest versus needing a complete change in stimulus?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tracking subjective factors like perceived exertion, sleep quality, and daily stress levels alongside objective metrics like heart rate variability can help differentiate mechanical fatigue from a harder CNS ceiling. If you see strength drop sharply after a heavy set but recover significantly by the next session, athlete fatigue is likely the driver, whereas a sustained inability to add volume or intensity despite rest or volume reduction often indicates a CNS hard ceiling requiring a full stimulus switch. To confirm this, try a 10-day cycle where one week deconstructs heavy neural loads to reset the CNS, followed immediately by a week with reduced volume but higher emphasis on speed, to see which variable breaks the deadlock. Distinguishing the two is crucial because treating a neuronal limit as simple fatigue will waste weeks, while misjudging fatigue as a plateau could lead to unnecessary dropout from a new program.",
      "source": "target",
